---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'schedule', title: 'Cross-Platform Scheduling' },
  { id: 'watch', title: 'Disk-Usage Watch' },
  { id: 'snapshot', title: 'Filesystem Snapshots' },
  { id: 'history-undo', title: 'History & Instant Undo' }
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Scheduling & Snapshots"
  description="Install unattended cleanup and memory tasks on the native scheduler, watch disk usage, manage snapshots, and undo any cleanup."
  currentSlug="automation"
  toc={toc}
  prevPage={{ title: 'Memory, Startup & Health', href: '/docs/system-tools' }}
  nextPage={{ title: 'Disk Intelligence Tools', href: '/docs/disk-intelligence' }}
>
  <div class="space-y-10">
    <!-- Schedule -->
    <section id="schedule">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cross-Platform Scheduling</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <code class="text-cyan font-mono">sweep schedule</code> installs an unattended cleanup on whichever scheduler the platform actually uses — no cron, and no third-party agent:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-4">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse bg-bg-surface border border-border rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-bg-elevated text-left text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-text-muted">
              <th class="p-3">Platform</th>
              <th class="p-3">Backend</th>
              <th class="p-3">Artifact</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-border/60 text-xs">
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono text-cyan">Linux</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-text-muted">systemd user timer</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono text-text-primary">sweep-clean.timer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono text-cyan">Windows</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-text-muted">Task Scheduler</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono text-text-primary">schtasks / Sweep\SweepClean</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono text-cyan">macOS</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-text-muted">launchd</td>
              <td class="p-3 font-mono text-text-primary">~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.sweep.clean.plist</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust"># Bare invocation prints the current status</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep schedule

<span class="text-rust"># Install a daily cleanup at 03:00</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep schedule --enable --hour 3 -- system.journal_vacuum apt.deep_cache

<span class="text-rust"># Also install the daily safe memory-trim task</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep schedule --enable --memopt

<span class="text-rust"># Remove it again</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep schedule --disable</code></pre>

      <div class="callout callout-tip mt-4">
        Non-interactive runs automatically capture an audit log and stay in the safe, non-shred mode. The Qt6/QML shell exposes the same thing on the Schedule screen, including a card for the memory task.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Watch -->
    <section id="watch">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Disk-Usage Watch</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        <code class="text-cyan font-mono">sweep watch</code> polls disk usage and alerts when a threshold is crossed. It can run as a daemon or in a single-shot, cron-friendly mode:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust"># Daemon: notify above 90% every 5 minutes</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep watch --threshold 90 --interval 300

<span class="text-rust"># Single check for a cron job — exits 3 when the alarm fires</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep watch --once --threshold 85

<span class="text-rust"># Include the five biggest files in the alarm</span>
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep watch --once --suggest 5</code></pre>
    </section>

    <!-- Snapshot -->
    <section id="snapshot">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Filesystem Snapshots</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        On systems with a snapshot manager, Sweep can take a checkpoint before a risky cleanup and roll back to it afterwards. It drives <code class="text-cyan">snapper</code> or <code class="text-cyan">timeshift</code> rather than reimplementing copy-on-write:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep snapshot create "before-cleanup"
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep snapshot list
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep snapshot rollback 42</code></pre>
      <p class="text-xs text-text-dim mt-3">
        macOS also has a dedicated Time Machine thinning provider (<code class="text-cyan">system.tm_thin</code>) for reclaiming local snapshot space.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- History & Undo -->
    <section id="history-undo">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-3">History &amp; Instant Undo</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Every destructive run appends to the operation log. <code class="text-cyan font-mono">sweep history</code> reads it back, and <code class="text-cyan font-mono">sweep undo</code> restores files when a <code class="text-cyan">--backup-dir</code> was used. Both share one log-line parser that reads fields from the right, so a path containing the field separator is still counted and restorable.
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block"><code><span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep history --days 7
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep undo
<span class="text-rust">$ </span>sweep undo --backup-dir ~/my-backups/sweep</code></pre>
      <p class="text-sm text-text-muted leading-relaxed mt-4">
        For one-off selections, <code class="text-cyan">--remember</code> saves the selection and <code class="text-cyan">--last</code> replays it; setting <code class="text-cyan">remember_last = true</code> in the config does it automatically for every clean.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
